<h1>Methods</h1>
<p class="lede">This is the whole protocol, including the parts of it that are weak. If you want to repeat it, everything you need is on this page.</p>

<h2 id="site">Site</h2>
<p>Station NBC-01 is the Nereid Boat Club dock in Rutherford, New Jersey, on the Lower Passaic. The published coordinate is 40.83 N, 74.11 W, rounded to two decimal places on purpose: one person is at the same place at the same hour every week, and the exact metre does not help anyone reading the data.</p>
<p>The reach is tidal. The Dundee Dam, about four miles up the river, is the head of tide, so the water at the dock moves both ways and what I catch depends on the stage. I record the stage every week and I do not correct for it. Depth at the dock end is a little over two metres at high water. The sample comes off the downstream corner of the float, where there is enough water under the bottle at any tide and enough room to work without standing in the way of a launching eight.</p>
<p>Sampling is fixed at Saturday, 08:15 to 08:45, before the club launches. A fixed hour is not a convenience. Bacteria counts in a tidal river move with the tide and with the sun, and a sample taken at 08:20 one week and 14:00 the next is two different measurements. Same dock, same corner, same half hour, every week.</p>
