<h2 id="collection">Collection</h2>
<ul class="bul">
<li>Sterile 100 mL bottle, or a Whirl-Pak, one per sample plus one for the duplicate check.</li>
<li>Gloves on before the bottle is opened.</li>
<li>The bottle goes in mouth down to about 30 cm, is turned to face into the current, and is opened under water. Nothing that floated past the dock five seconds ago goes in the bottle.</li>
<li>Filled to the line with no headspace, capped under water, then out.</li>
<li>On ice immediately, in a cooler with a thermometer in it, and processed within 6 hours. Six hours is the standard holding time for recreational bacteria samples. In practice it is under two, because the plates go in at the kitchen table by 09:30.</li>
<li>A field blank, sterile water carried through every step, once a month. If a blank grows anything, that month's numbers get flagged on the record.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="analysis">Analysis</h2>
<p>The method is Coliscan Easygel from Micrology Laboratories, which is the same method most volunteer monitoring programs use because it needs no incubator you cannot build and no autoclave at all. 5 mL of sample goes into a bottle of medium, the bottle is swirled, and the contents are poured into a pretreated dish where the medium sets. The dish goes into the incubator at 35 degrees C for 24 hours.</p>
<p>Color is the whole reading, and the manufacturer's rule is specific: purple through dark blue colonies are E. coli and get counted. Pink, red and magenta colonies are other coliforms, written on the sheet and left out of the number on this site. Teal, blue green and white colonies are counted as nothing at all. Every sample is plated in duplicate and the reported value is the mean of the two plates.</p>
<p>The arithmetic is deliberately simple: colonies on one 5 mL plate multiplied by 20 gives CFU per 100 mL. Because the reported value is the mean of two plates, every number on this site is a multiple of 20. That is a property of the method, not rounding.</p>
