<h2 id="parameters">Field parameters</h2>
<p>Seven things get written on the sheet at the dock before the bottle goes in the cooler: water temperature in degrees C, pH, turbidity in NTU by turbidity tube, nitrate as N in mg/L, orthophosphate in mg/L, tide stage, and the 48 hour antecedent rainfall in inches.</p>
<p>The rainfall is read off my own gauge at the boathouse. It is not a National Weather Service number and it is not a CoCoRaHS number, and I do not want anyone treating it as one. A gauge at the dock is the right measurement for what happens at the dock, and cross checking it against a nearby official record is on the list of things to do next.</p>

<h2 id="criteria">The criteria, and which one this advisory uses</h2>
<p>Three published numbers matter here and they are not the same number.</p>
<ul class="bul">
<li><b>126 CFU/100 mL</b> is the geometric mean criterion for E. coli in fresh primary contact recreation waters, from EPA's 1986 bacteria criteria and carried forward as a recommended geometric mean in the 2012 Recreational Water Quality Criteria.</li>
<li><b>235 CFU/100 mL</b> is EPA's Beach Action Value for culturable E. coli in fresh water in that 2012 document, the value EPA recommends states use for beach notification. It is the same order as the 1986 single sample maximum for a designated bathing beach.</li>
<li><b>New Jersey's own standard</b> is different again. N.J.A.C. 7:9B-1.14 sets, for FW2 primary contact recreation, an E. coli geometric mean of 100 per 100 mL over a 90 day period and a statistical threshold value of 320 per 100 mL that should not be exceeded by more than ten percent of samples.</li>
</ul>
<p>This advisory calls red above 235. The reason is practical: the state's numbers are a 90 day geometric mean and an excursion frequency, and neither can be evaluated from one grab sample on a Saturday morning. 235 is a single sample value, published for exactly this purpose, and a rower standing on a dock at eight in the morning needs a single sample value. The 126 line is drawn on the season chart as well, because the season geometric mean is the number the state standard is actually comparable to.</p>
<p><b>So two lines run through this site and they do different jobs.</b> <b>235</b> is the line the advisory calls red at, and it is the line the loading model solves for on the <a class='tx' href='/model/'>model page</a>. <b>126</b> is the line a Saturday has to stay under to be called green, and it is therefore the line the rain and risk model is scored against below. A count of 200 is over one line and under the other, which is exactly why every mention of either number on this site names it instead of saying "the line".</p>
<p>Both citations are in the sources at the bottom of this page with retrieval dates. If you think I have read either of them wrong, write to me and say so.</p>

<h2 id="validation">Validation</h2>
<p>Three of the ten samples were split in the field and half of each was sent to an NJDEP certified commercial laboratory, which ran them by Colilert-18 and Quanti-Tray, an EPA approved most probable number method described in Standard Methods 9223B. The pairs are below.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="tbl tight">
<caption>Split samples, field against laboratory</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th class="n" scope="col">Field, CFU/100 mL</th><th class="n" scope="col">Laboratory, MPN/100 mL</th><th class="n" scope="col">RPD</th><th class="n" scope="col">log10 apart</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>29 August 2026</td><td class="n">980</td><td class="n">1,120</td><td class="n">13.3%</td><td class="n">0.058</td></tr><tr><td>19 September 2026</td><td class="n">2,400</td><td class="n">2,010</td><td class="n">17.7%</td><td class="n">0.077</td></tr><tr><td>10 October 2026</td><td class="n">640</td><td class="n">720</td><td class="n">11.8%</td><td class="n">0.051</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Mean relative percent difference 14.3%, and every pair agrees within 0.08 log10. An environmental laboratory would call anything inside 0.5 log10 acceptable agreement for a microbiological split, so these are comfortably inside it. Field duplicates agreed within 20 percent every week.</p>
<p>What three splits establish: the field method is not systematically reading high or low against a certified laboratory over the range this river produces. What they do not establish: a quality assurance program. Three pairs cannot characterize variance, they cannot catch a bad batch of medium, and they were all taken by the same person on the same dock. Ten splits across two samplers would be worth more than thirty of mine.</p>


<h2 id="model">Rain and risk</h2>
<p>The advisory reports a measurement that is already six days old by the time it reaches the list on Friday. So the second question is the useful one: given the rain since Thursday, how likely is tomorrow to be over the 235 line.</p>
<p>I fitted a logistic regression for that. It is trained on published agency data for this reach, not on my own samples: 690 E. coli results from three New Jersey Harbor Dischargers Group stations, NJHDG-5 (Dundee Dam), NJHDG-7 (Union Avenue), NJHDG-8 (Rutgers Street), collected between February 2011 and December 2019 and pulled from the Water Quality Portal. Antecedent rainfall for those historical samples comes from a published daily precipitation series for the reach, summed over the two days before each sample. The model predicts the probability that a sample is over 235 CFU/100 mL from the log of that antecedent rainfall and a warm season term. On a temporal holdout, fitted on 2011 to 2016 and tested on 175 later observations, it reaches an area under the ROC curve of 0.70. That is a real relationship and a weak one, which is what the scatter of river bacteria data looks like.</p>
<p>The agency stations run much dirtier than the dock. Union Avenue, the nearest one, has a dry weather geometric mean of 397 CFU/100 mL across 181 samples against 112 at NBC-01. So the rainfall slope transfers and the level does not: I keep the fitted slope, 1.66 per log10 inch, and set the intercept at this station. The curve then crosses the decision line at 0.4 in of antecedent rain.</p>
<p>My ten Saturdays are the held out test, and n = 10 is worth saying out loud. Each Saturday is scored on one question: did that Saturday need an advisory above green, which is to say did the count reach 126. The model called 9 of the ten correctly, missing only 24 October, when it asked for caution and the count came back at 120, six under the 126 line. The rule of thumb it is competing with, treat the water as unsafe if the gauge caught more than half an inch in the last two days, called 8 of the ten: it missed 22 August and 5 September, two dry mornings that came back in the caution band, and a false green is the expensive way to be wrong. The looser version of the same rule, any measurable rain of 0.10 in or more, adds a third error on 24 October, which had 0.12 in of rain and a count of 120. Neither rule got 26 September wrong on this test: both called red, the water came back yellow at 200, and an over-call by one band is the safe direction to be wrong in.</p>
