"none" stroke="#C9CDC6" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="534.0" y="261.0" font-family="IBM Plex Sans, Helvetica, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#4A555C" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" letter-spacing="0">called clear</text></svg></div><figcaption><b>Left:</b> the fitted probability of exceeding 235 CFU/100 mL against antecedent rainfall, with the ten dock Saturdays as ticks on the axis. <b>Right:</b> what each rule called on the red question, over 235 CFU/100 mL, week by week, with the rain rule at its loosest, any measurable rain. A cross is a week called wrong on that test, which is why 26 September carries one in both rows. The advisory test scored in the text above is a different and lower bar, 126, so it turns on different weeks. Training set 690 published observations; held out set 10 dock samples. A model validated on ten points is a hypothesis, not a result.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Two honest limits. Ten is a small held out set and one different Saturday would move the score by ten percentage points. And the error costs are not symmetric: a missed exceedance puts someone's hands in sewage, a false red costs a rower a bit of caution, so the decision line sits where a false red is the cheaper mistake.</p>
<p>The 48 hour window is not something I discovered. It is the window published guidance already uses for contact recreation after wet weather. What the model adds is where the rainfall line goes for this stretch of river, which turned out to be about four tenths of an inch rather than any rain at all.</p>
<p>This one is a correlation and it says nothing about why. The <a class='tx' href='/model/'>loading model</a> is the other half: a mass balance for the reach between the dam and the dock, with the die-off rate swept across its published range, asking how long the water actually stays over the 235 line after an overflow stops and whether 48 hours is long enough.</p>


<h2 id="limits">What this cannot tell you</h2>
<ul class="bul">
<li>One grab sample, one point, one tide stage, once a week. The river at 08:20 on Saturday is not the river at 16:00 on Wednesday.</li>
<li>Easygel is a presence and count method with real variability, and it gets less precise as counts get high. A plate with 120 colonies on it is near the top of what I can count honestly, so the largest numbers here should be read as an order of magnitude.</li>
<li>No flow normalization. A wet Saturday and a dry Saturday are not the same river, and I report them side by side anyway.</li>
<li>E. coli is an indicator, not the thing you would actually get sick from. It tells you fecal contamination is present; it does not identify a source, and it says nothing about the metals and dioxins the Lower Passaic is better known for.</li>
<li>No sampling in lightning, in ice, or when the club has closed the dock. A missed week would be recorded as a gap, not filled in.</li>
</ul>

